<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marin Držić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1508–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marin Držić was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> playwright and poet from the Republic of Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>), regarded as the greatest Croatian writer of comedies. His best-known play, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundo-maroje">Dundo Maroje</a> (c. 1551), satirises greed and old age in the manner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plautus">Plautus</a>. Late in life he sought Medici support for a plot against Ragusa's aristocratic government, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1567.</p></div>
